<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poseidon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Neptune (Roman)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Poseidon was the Greek god of the sea, earthquakes and horses, brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>. He was one of the most important gods of the Mycenaean world, receiving many offerings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a> tablets of Pylos. Sanctuaries such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> and Cape Sounion were dedicated to him, and the Greek colony of Poseidonia in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paestum">Paestum</a>, was named after him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> identified him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/poseidon.jpg" alt="Poseidon" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: —DerHexer (Talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Poseidon_NAMA_235_(DerHexer),_part_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
